course: dictionary
lesson: 15201
title: Choosing carefully: selective
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Selective means choosing carefully, so you take some things and leave others. «Mia was selective about which books she borrowed, picking only the ones about space.» She did not take every book. She picked only some of them.
explain: Selective can also describe a school. «A selective school only takes pupils who pass a test.» The school chooses who can join, so not everyone gets in. Selective breeding works in the same way: people choose which animals or plants will have young.
explain: Context helps you check the meaning. «The weedkiller is selective, so it kills the weeds but leaves the grass alone.» Here selective means it affects only some things. Try swapping in picky: the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Selective means choosing carefully and taking only some things.
fact: A selective school only takes pupils who pass a test or meet certain standards.
fact: In the UK, grammar schools are selective schools that take pupils after the 11-plus test.
fact: Selective breeding means choosing which animals or plants have young, so good features are passed on, as in dog breeds or bigger crops.
fact: «Selective hearing» is a joke about only hearing what you want to hear.
exercise: «Mia was selective about which books she borrowed.» What does selective mean here? | Losing books on the bus | Borrowing every book in the library | Reading very fast | *Choosing carefully, taking only some
exercise: «A selective school only takes pupils who pass a test.» Who can join this school? | Only the teachers' children | Everyone who lives nearby | Anyone who asks | *Only pupils who pass the test
exercise: «The farmer was selective, choosing only the strongest hens to have chicks.» Find and copy the word that means choosing carefully. | *selective | chicks | farmer | strongest
test: «The weedkiller is selective: it kills the weeds but leaves the grass.» What does selective suggest here? | It makes grass grow faster | It is a kind of flower | It affects everything in the garden | *It affects only some things
test: Sam says he has «selective hearing» when he ignores his mum. What does it mean? | He hears better than anyone | He hears only music | He cannot hear at all | *He only hears what he wants to hear
exam: Which is the best example of selective breeding? | A cat sleeping in the sun | Rain falling on a field | A child choosing a sandwich for lunch | *Farmers choosing which cows have calves, so the best features are passed on
